#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110383 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5050393b-0b84-58ad-8e86-fcb53e8add8e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Measure of Vertical Angles Given in Terms of a Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "d8d0babe-ca51-5d80-ba9c-5289697cb968" :question/math-academy-id "e-2579" :question/problem "Compute the measures of the shaded angles in the figure below.\n\n![](images/6d/6d39f6b553856204eb1958c84fb06dc2756edb4e44beee4c55550aad8d3592f4.png)" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical. Since vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3x - 15^{∘} &= 2x + 10^{∘} \\\\\nx - 15^{∘} &= 10^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 25^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=25^\\circ$, we can substitute into one of the expressions to calculate the measure of the angles. Let's substitute into the expression $2x + 10^\\circ$:\n\n$$\n2x + 10^\\circ = 2 \\cdot 25^\\circ + 10^\\circ = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nTherefore, both angles have a measure of $60^\\circ$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110384 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110751 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f0fce9a0-b7fc-51b1-9335-f8853d0d8fe9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Unknown Angle by Combining Properties of Straight and Vertical Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "02660015-d4fd-53f3-b7a5-4a4118e406b3" :question/math-academy-id "e-2583" :question/problem "In the figure below, $\\angle AOB$ is a straight angle. Determine $m \\angle AOD$.\n\n![](images/6e/6eac6c11dcc513cdf896cdfaa9650689af1bf58afd147783d0bb1ca61dd3a324.png)" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\angle AOB$ is straight,\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOB = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSo by the angle addition postulate, we have\n\n$$\nm\\angle AOC + m\\angle BOC = 180^\\circ\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given expressions into the above, we can solve for $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(5x + 100^{∘}) + 11x &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n16x + 100^{∘} &= 180^{∘} \\\\\n16x &= 80^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 5^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we know $x=5^\\circ$, we can calculate $m \\angle COB$ as follows:\n\n$$\nm \\angle COB = 11(5^\\circ) = 55^\\circ\n$$\n\nFinally, since the angles $\\angle AOD$ and $\\angle COB$ are vertical angles, they have the same measure:\n\n$$\nm \\angle AOD = m \\angle COB = 55^\\circ\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110385 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110753 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "44a00376-bac8-5b09-baff-5dfd613c3222" :knowledge-point/title "Using Vertical Angles in a Situation With Three Lines"}] :question/id #uuid "a5dae4ad-b516-5ece-8d12-5805c5073fe9" :question/math-academy-id "e-2587" :question/problem "In the diagram shown below, it is known that $m \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ$ and $m \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ$. Determine the value of $m \\angle 2 + m \\angle 4$.\n\n![](images/1b/1bc662904a52724fac28dc180f70088592ff4da181930c6607b1bb590b3ec188.png)" :question/worked-solution "We have two pairs of vertical angles, and we know that vertical angles are congruent and therefore have the same measure. So, we have\n\n$$\nm∠1 = m∠2,; m∠3 = m∠4\n$$\n\nWe are told that\n\n$$\nm \\angle 1 = 60^\\circ\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nm \\angle 3 = 120^\\circ\n$$\n\nso we have\n\n$$\n60^{∘} = m∠2,; 120^{∘} = m∠4\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm∠2 + m∠4 &= 60^{∘} + 120^{∘} \\\\\n&= 180^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110436 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110547 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f6b7437-6922-58bd-a819-de7fb02a261e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating an Unknown Quantity Using Vertical Angles"}] :question/id #uuid "11f90b96-0b4d-5f8d-8b0d-b4cde31fc7fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-3044" :question/problem "Given the figure below, find the value of $x$.\n\n![](images/8b/8b89eec86675b2bebfda74ecf1da8e0b7980e5de6d3a52de84e93e64a4a62068.png)" :question/worked-solution "The two angles marked in the diagram are vertical.\nSince vertical angles are congruent, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x + 12^{∘} &= 54^{∘} \\\\\n2x &= 42^{∘} \\\\\nx &= 21^{∘}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
